@excalidraw/excalidraw 0.18.1 + react 18 + react-dom 18 — prebuilt IIFE bundle
================================================================================

Rebuilt 2026-11-03. The first build was broken; see "WHY IT WAS REBUILT".

WHY A PREBUILT BUNDLE
=====================
`@excalidraw/excalidraw`'s `dist/prod` is **not** standalone. Its `index.js`
imports `react`, `react-dom`, `jotai`, `roughjs`, `nanoid`, `clsx`, `fuzzy`,
`open-color`, `tunnel-rat` and more by bare specifier — it is built for a
bundler. Odoo 18 ships **Owl**, not React, so React cannot be borrowed from
the backend.

So the bundle is built here and committed.

WHY IT WAS REBUILT
==================
The first build used `--global-name=ExcalidrawLib`. esbuild then emits

    var ExcalidrawLib = (() => { ... })();

and the entry file ALSO assigns `window.ExcalidrawLib` inside that IIFE. The
top-level `var` shadows the assignment: the IIFE returns undefined, the var
is set to undefined, and `window.ExcalidrawLib` stays undefined. The page
died on `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'Excalidraw')` with a
blank canvas.

The first build also declared `window.React` / `window.ReactDOM` in this file
as if the bundle exposed them. It did not — the entry only set
`window.ExcalidrawLib = { Excalidraw }`.

Both are fixed:
  * no `--global-name` — the IIFE is anonymous, so nothing shadows the global
  * the entry assigns `window.React` and `window.ReactDOM` explicitly
  * `import * as` + `unwrap()` instead of relying on `__toESM(..., 1)`, which
    nulls `.default` for these CJS packages

BUILD COMMAND
-------------
    npm init -y
    npm install @excalidraw/excalidraw@0.18.1 react@18 react-dom@18 esbuild

    cat > entry.jsx <<'EOF'
    import * as ExcalidrawPkg from "@excalidraw/excalidraw";
    import * as ReactPkg from "react";
    import * as ReactDOMPkg from "react-dom/client";
    import "@excalidraw/excalidraw/index.css";

    function unwrap(m) { return m && m.default ? m.default : m; }

    var React = unwrap(ReactPkg);
    var ReactDOM = unwrap(ReactDOMPkg);
    var Excalidraw =
      ExcalidrawPkg.Excalidraw ||
      (ExcalidrawPkg.default && ExcalidrawPkg.default.Excalidraw);

    window.ExcalidrawLib = { Excalidraw: Excalidraw };
    window.React = React;
    window.ReactDOM = ReactDOM;
    EOF

    ./node_modules/.bin/esbuild entry.jsx \
      --bundle --format=iife \
      --outfile=bundle.js \
      --loader:.woff2=file --loader:.woff=file --loader:.ttf=file \
      --asset-names=assets/[name]-[hash] \
      --define:process.env.NODE_ENV='"production"' \
      --conditions=production

    # bundle.js  → static/lib/excalidraw/excalidraw.bundle.js
    # bundle.css → static/lib/excalidraw/excalidraw.bundle.css
    # assets/    → static/lib/excalidraw/assets/

NOTE: `--conditions=production` is required — without it the CSS import from
`@excalidraw/excalidraw/index.css` does not resolve and the build fails.

FILE SIZES
----------
    excalidraw.bundle.js    14 MB   (React and ReactDOM are now explicit)
    excalidraw.bundle.css   161 KB
    assets/                 80 KB  (4 Assistant woff2 files)

The first build was 8.4 MB because React was pulled in through the package's
own imports rather than as an explicit dependency.

WHY 0.18.1 AND NOT 19.0.0
=========================
`19.0.0` appears in the running container's bundle, but it is **React's**
version (`st.version="19.0.0"` next to `useSyncExternalStore`), not
Excalidraw's. `npm view @excalidraw/excalidraw versions` shows the latest
published package is `0.18.1`. Verified 2026-11-02.

THE BUNDLE MUST BE COMMITTED
============================
It is built outside the repo and copied in. On 2026-11-03 a deploy of the
module tarball replaced the working 14 MB bundle with the old broken 8.4 MB
one, and the only symptom was a blank canvas with no console error. Keeping
the built file in `static/lib/excalidraw/` means every deploy ships the same
bytes.

UPGRADING
---------
  1. npm install @excalidraw/excalidraw@<version> react@18 react-dom@18
  2. rebuild with the command above
  3. copy bundle.js, bundle.css and assets/ here
  4. update this file
  5. verify in a browser: `window.ExcalidrawLib.Excalidraw` must be an object,
     and the canvas must render

